p.src:
Since $H$ sends $\ket{\psi_E}$ back into the same one-dimensional direction, time evolution cannot mix it with another eigenvector. It can only multiply it by a scalar: $\ket{\psi_E(t)}=f(t)\ket{\psi_E}.$ Substitution into the Schrödinger equation gives $\begin{aligned}i\hbar\,\dot f(t)\ket{\psi_E}
&=
f(t)H\ket{\psi_E} \\
&=
E f(t)\ket{\psi_E}.\end{aligned}$ Therefore $\dot f(t)=-\frac{iE}{\hbar}f(t),
\qquad
f(t)=e^{-iEt/\hbar},$ and $\boxed{
\ket{\psi_E(t)}
=e^{-iEt/\hbar}\ket{\psi_E(0)}
}.$ An energy eigenstate is stationary in the sense that its ray in Hilbert space does not move. Its ket nevertheless accumulates phase.

